WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.900 --> 00:00:04.632
Take payments on PageMotor with Stripe and Claude Code.

2
00:00:04.632 --> 00:00:17.525
Set Stripe up properly, let Claude Code do the wiring from your terminal, and turn a PageMotor site into one that actually takes money. Test mode first, and a real card only at the very end.

3
00:00:18.225 --> 00:00:25.984
Three parts do three different jobs here, and it is worth knowing which is which before anything goes wrong.

4
00:00:25.984 --> 00:00:37.191
Stripe is the payment processor and dashboard: it holds your products, prices, customers and payouts, and it is where you create keys and register webhooks.

5
00:00:37.191 --> 00:00:43.442
Claude Code operates Stripe for you from the terminal, through Stripe's own MCP server.

6
00:00:43.442 --> 00:00:57.235
And PageMotor, through the EP Ecommerce Stripe plugin, renders the checkout, verifies the webhook signature and records the order. That is the part that actually takes the money on your pages.

7
00:00:57.935 --> 00:01:04.735
Step one is creating the account, and there is one question in it that carries more weight than the rest.

8
00:01:04.735 --> 00:01:16.458
Sign-up asks where your business is legally registered. That choice sets your currency and your payout rules, it is tied to your legal entity, and you cannot freely switch it later.

9
00:01:16.458 --> 00:01:26.821
Note too that availability depends on where your business is established, not where your customers are. Ireland and the United Kingdom are both fully supported.

10
00:01:27.521 --> 00:01:33.846
Step two, the keys. There are three kinds, and the prefix on each one tells you what it is.

11
00:01:33.846 --> 00:01:41.979
A publishable key, p k underscore, is safe in the browser: it is how Stripe's own JavaScript identifies your account.

12
00:01:41.979 --> 00:01:53.032
A secret key, s k underscore, is server-side only and has unrestricted access to your entire account, so it never goes in front-end code and never goes in git.

13
00:01:53.032 --> 00:02:02.902
And a restricted key, r k underscore, carries only the permissions you grant it, per resource. That is the one you hand to a tool or an agent.

14
00:02:02.902 --> 00:02:11.313
Every one of them also carries test or live in the middle, which is how you spot a live key in a test script at a glance.

15
00:02:12.013 --> 00:02:23.087
Step three is the one that actually wires it up, and it is a single line. Stripe hosts an official MCP server, and Claude Code connects to it over HTTP.

16
00:02:23.087 --> 00:02:28.551
Add it, then run slash m c p inside Claude Code to complete authentication.

17
00:02:28.551 --> 00:02:42.757
There are two ways to authenticate, and on your own machine the first is the one to pick: OAuth in the browser. You approve on a Stripe consent screen, and no key is stored in your config at all.

18
00:02:43.457 --> 00:02:57.892
A short warning about something you will very likely read elsewhere. Plenty of pages still show a tools flag on the local Stripe MCP package, telling you to list which tools the agent gets. That flag does not exist any more.

19
00:02:57.892 --> 00:03:06.333
What controls the tools now is the set of permissions on the restricted key you pass. Scope the key, and you have scoped the agent.

20
00:03:06.333 --> 00:03:14.711
Nothing else decides it, so if a page is still showing you that flag, it is out of date and probably wrong about other things too.

21
00:03:15.411 --> 00:03:26.823
Step four, and the order here matters. Ask for something read-only first: list my Stripe products and prices. Real data coming back is what proves the connection is live.

22
00:03:26.823 --> 00:03:34.341
Then try a small, reversible write: create a test product called Sample Download, then show me how to delete it.

23
00:03:34.341 --> 00:03:51.123
Claude will tell you what it is about to do before it touches anything. Read that plan, and then approve. Keep that confirmation switched on for every write action, because it is your safety net while you learn what the connection can actually reach.

24
00:03:51.823 --> 00:04:05.483
Step five is the payoff, on the PageMotor side. Two plugins need to be active: EP Ecommerce, which is the store engine, and EP Ecommerce Stripe, the payment provider that extends it.

25
00:04:05.483 --> 00:04:14.189
Then open Settings, EP Ecommerce Stripe. Tick Enable Stripe Payments, set Mode to Test, and paste your test keys in.

26
00:04:14.189 --> 00:04:23.495
The plugin selects the active key by mode at runtime, so your live keys sit there unused until you deliberately switch over.

27
00:04:24.195 --> 00:04:30.966
Now the webhook, which is the thing that makes a payment real on your site rather than just in the browser.

28
00:04:30.966 --> 00:04:42.168
When a payment succeeds, Stripe notifies your site server to server, instead of your site trusting a message from the customer's browser. That is the authoritative confirmation.

29
00:04:42.168 --> 00:04:49.952
The plugin shows you the exact endpoint URL to register, built from your own site address, and it listens for three events.

30
00:04:49.952 --> 00:05:07.291
And here is the part that catches people: the signing secret is a third distinct secret. It is not an API key, and every endpoint has its own. Paste the one matching the endpoint and the mode you are actually using, or signature verification fails and orders never complete.

31
00:05:07.991 --> 00:05:13.636
And finally, the step that proves the whole chain is real rather than merely configured.

32
00:05:13.636 --> 00:05:21.014
Pay with Stripe's test card, four two four two, repeated, with any future expiry and any three digit security code.

33
00:05:21.014 --> 00:05:32.882
Then read the order back in both places: the order recorded in your PageMotor admin, and the payment in your Stripe dashboard. If both show it, the webhook fired and the loop is closed.

34
00:05:32.882 --> 00:05:38.270
That round trip is the proof. Anything short of it is just a setting you have saved.

